    "['term [parameter2] from protocol [parameter3]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'route-map IMPORT-CONNECTED permit 10', 'parent_command': ['router bgp 100'], 'root': 'router bgp 100', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\nroute-map IMPORT-CONNECTED permit 10\\n match protocol connected\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- Juniper\\'s `policy-statement` translates to Cisco\\'s `route-map`\\n- The `term 1` becomes the sequence number `10` (Cisco typically uses multiples of 10)\\n- `from protocol direct` becomes `match protocol connected` (Juniper\\'s \"direct\" = Cisco\\'s \"connected\")\\n- The implicit `then accept` is represented by the `permit` keyword in the route-map statement', 'trans_template': 'route-map IMPORT-CONNECTED permit 10'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'match protocol connected', 'parent_command': ['router bgp 100'], 'root': 'router bgp 100', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\nroute-map IMPORT-CONNECTED permit 10\\n match protocol connected\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- Juniper\\'s `policy-statement` translates to Cisco\\'s `route-map`\\n- The `term 1` becomes the sequence number `10` (Cisco typically uses multiples of 10)\\n- `from protocol direct` becomes `match protocol connected` (Juniper\\'s \"direct\" = Cisco\\'s \"connected\")\\n- The implicit `then accept` is represented by the `permit` keyword in the route-map statement', 'trans_template': 'match protocol connected'}]]": 1,
